One likely culprit jumps out from the script: your target PCA
coefficients aren't standardized. Look at this line:

  targetCoeffs = encodeSpectral(btPCA, btRaw, false);   % <- standardize=false
  
PCA coefficients have wildly different natural scales — the 1st
component (most variance) can be 10-100x larger in magnitude than the
15th or 30th. Plain MSE on unstandardized coefficients means the loss
is almost entirely dominated by getting the first couple of components
right; the network has very little gradient pressure to fit the later
components well, and those later components still matter for
channel-level accuracy. This can produce exactly the kind of uniform,
unstructured ~1 K floor you're seeing rather than a spiky,
spectrally-localized error.

Fix :  standardize both target and prediction:

  targetCoeffs = encodeSpectral(btPCA, btRaw, true);   % standardize=true now

Then at evaluation time, un-standardize before decoding back to BT:

  predCoeffsVal = extractdata(forwardMLP(params, dlarray(single(Xval)','CB')))';
  btReconVal    = decodeSpectral(btPCA, predCoeffsVal, true);   % standardize=true here too, matches training

Do the same in the train-set reconstruction check you just ran.

Second thing to check before adding capacity: did training actually
converge, or did you just run out of epochs? Track and plot the loss
curve — right now you're only printing every 5th epoch, so you can't
