On standardization backfiring — that can genuinely happen here, and
it's worth knowing which of two things caused it:

1. A wiring mismatch is the more common cause. Standardizing requires
consistency in exactly two places: the targetCoeffs you train against,
and the decodeSpectral(...) call at evaluation time. If you flipped
standardize=true on the encode side but left the decode call at false
(or vice versa), you're comparing/reconstructing on the wrong scale
and everything degrades. Worth double-checking both calls use the same
flag before concluding standardization itself is the problem.

2. If the wiring is right, this is a real and known failure mode of
standardizing PCA coefficients. The last several of your 32 components
carry very little true signal — mostly noise, by construction (that's
why they're last in variance order). Standardizing gives every
component equal weight in the loss regardless of how much real
information it holds, which forces the network to spend capacity
chasing noise in weakly-informative components at the expense of
fitting the dominant, physically meaningful ones well. With a network
that was already borderline on capacity/convergence (which increasing
epochs just confirmed), adding that burden can easily make things
worse rather than better.

