A capacity increase doing essentially nothing (128→512 neurons, 2→3
layers, no real movement) is itself diagnostic — it's telling you the
bottleneck almost certainly isn't network capacity anymore. That's
actually useful information: it means more layers/neurons is very
unlikely to be the right next move, and you should stop spending time
there. A few different directions worth checking, roughly in order of
how cheap they are to test:

1. Recheck the truncation floor for your current configuration, not
the original one. You checked 0.086 K truncation floor a while back,
but that was before the sigma-level rework and before channel
subsetting. If you're now testing on the 1300-1700 cm⁻¹ band
specifically, the truncation floor for that subset with that nPCA_BT
could be different — a narrower band with sharper features doesn't
necessarily compress as cleanly. This is the cheapest thing to rule
back in or out:

  %% this is in do_stats
  btPCA_current = fitSpectralPCA(btRaw_currentConfig, nPCA_BT);
  roundTrip = decodeSpectral(btPCA_current, encodeSpectral(btPCA_current, btRaw_currentConfig, false), false);
  truncRMSE = sqrt(mean((roundTrip - btRaw_currentConfig).^2, 1));
  fprintf('Current-config truncation floor: mean %.3f K\n', mean(truncRMSE));

If this comes back near 0.5 K, you've found it — bump nPCA_BT, done.

2. Fit a plain linear regression baseline from the same inputs to the
same PCA-coefficient targets. This is the most informative test given
where you are: it tells you whether the residual you're stuck at is a
genuinely nonlinear relationship the network should be able to capture
(in which case a linear fit does noticeably worse than your DNN), or
whether the DNN is basically just rediscovering a near-linear fit and
the ~0.5 K floor is something linear regression hits too — which would
point away from "needs more capacity" entirely and toward missing
information or noise.

  % closed-form ridge regression, X: (nTrain x nFeatures), Y: (nTrain x nPCA_BT)
  lambda = 1e-3;
  Wridge = (Xtrain'*Xtrain + lambda*eye(size(Xtrain,2))) \ (Xtrain'*Ytrain);
  predLinearVal = Xval * Wridge;
  linRMSE = sqrt(mean((predLinearVal - Yval).^2, 'all'));
  fprintf('Linear baseline val MSE (coeff space): %.5f\n', linRMSE);

If linear regression gets you close to 0.5 K too, your DNN isn't
underperforming — the ceiling is coming from somewhere else (see #3,
#4).


3. Check whether the bigger networks actually converged, or just
stalled at a similar point. "No significant change" between 128 and
512 neurons could mean the extra capacity genuinely isn't needed, or
it could mean the bigger networks need more epochs / a lower learning
rate to actually use that capacity, and you're comparing two
under-trained runs. Plot the train loss curve for the 512-neuron run
specifically — is it still visibly decreasing at your last epoch, or
flat?

  plot(1:numEpochs, trainLossHistory); xlabel('epoch'); ylabel('train MSE');

Bigger networks often need a learning-rate schedule (decay learnRate
by, say, 0.5x every N epochs when val loss plateaus) rather than a
fixed rate the whole way through — worth adding if the curve looks
like it's oscillating rather than settling.

4. Check for label inconsistency: near-duplicate inputs with different
outputs. If your RT forward model has any stochastic component, or if
two profiles that are very close in your compressed feature space (X)
correspond to meaningfully different radiances (Y), that puts a hard
floor on any regression — network or linear — regardless of
capacity. Quick check: find the nearest-neighbor pairs in X-space and
look at the spread in their corresponding Y:

  sampleIdx = randperm(size(Xtrain,1), 200);
  nnIdx = knnsearch(Xtrain, Xtrain(sampleIdx,:), 'K', 2);  % 2nd column = nearest neighbor (excl self)
  pairDiffY = Ytrain(sampleIdx,:) - Ytrain(nnIdx(:,2),:);
  fprintf('Nearest-neighbor input pairs, output std: %.4f\n', std(pairDiffY(:)));

If nearest neighbors in input space still differ substantially in
output, that's your floor — no amount of network capacity fixes it,
only better/denoised training data would.

5. Consider whether view angle needs to interact multiplicatively
rather than just being concatenated. Optical path length scales with
secant(angle) multiplying the whole absorption profile — that's a
multiplicative interaction between one scalar and every level of every
gas profile, which a plain concatenated MLP has to learn indirectly
and can struggle with even at high capacity. If you haven't tried it,
an easy test is adding an explicit engineered feature — e.g., the
profile scaled by secant, or secant × total column water vapor — as an
extra input, rather than relying on the network to discover the
interaction on its own.

I'd start with #1 and #2 — they're both cheap (no retraining needed)
and will tell you immediately whether you're chasing a capacity
problem (you're not, if the answer to #2 is "linear does about as
well") or something else entirely.


