Given the overfitting signature, here's what to try, roughly in order
of effort:

  Go back down in size, not up. 1024×3 on 38k samples was always going
  to be capacity-rich; you already had a working, much smaller point
  (128-256, 2 layers) with train≈val. The fix for overfitting is
  essentially never "add more capacity" — go back to something like
  128-192 wide, 2 layers, and put the effort into #2 and #3 instead.

  Add early stopping via checkpointing on best val loss (from a couple
  messages back) if you haven't wired it in yet — with train/val
  diverging this much, you're very likely using a badly-overfit
  final-epoch model when an earlier epoch would generalize
  better. This is close to free and should be done regardless of
  anything else.
  
  Add L2 weight decay or dropout. At 1024 neurons especially, some
  regularization is close to mandatory. Dropout is easy to add to a
  manual dlarray forward pass:

    h1 = relu(fullyconnect(X, params.fc1_W, params.fc1_b));
    h1 = h1 .* (rand(size(h1),'like',h1) > dropoutRate) / (1-dropoutRate);  % only during training

  Start with dropoutRate = 0.2-0.3 on the hidden layers, none on the input or output.

  Check for train/val leakage. Given your profiles were "chosen for
  variability" rather than randomly sampled, it's worth confirming
